body:
Cursor pagination filters only by `created_at > cursor.created_at` while ordering solely by `created_at`. If multiple conversations share the same `created_at` timestamp, records can be skipped or duplicated across pages. Use a stable composite ordering (e.g., `created_at` + `id`) and a matching cursor filter (e.g., `created_at > ts OR (created_at = ts AND id > cursorId)`).
```suggestion
    const baseFilters: Record<string, unknown> = {};
    if (options.userId) baseFilters.user_id = options.userId;
    if (options.agentId) baseFilters.agent_id = options.agentId;

    // Cursor-based pagination: cursor is a conversation ID.
    // Fetch the cursor conversation's created_at to build a stable composite cursor
    // using (created_at, id) so pagination is deterministic even for identical timestamps.
    if (options.cursor) {
      const cursorRow = await this.engine.findOne(CONVERSATIONS_OBJECT, {
        where: { id: options.cursor },
        fields: ['created_at'],
      });
      if (cursorRow) {
        const paginationFilter = {
          $or: [
            // All conversations strictly after the cursor's created_at
            { created_at: { $gt: cursorRow.created_at } },
            // Conversations with the same created_at but a greater id
            {
              created_at: cursorRow.created_at,
              id: { $gt: options.cursor },
            },
          ],
        };

        if (Object.keys(baseFilters).length > 0) {
          where.$and = [baseFilters, paginationFilter];
        } else {
          Object.assign(where, paginationFilter);
        }
      } else if (Object.keys(baseFilters).length > 0) {
        Object.assign(where, baseFilters);
      }
    } else if (Object.keys(baseFilters).length > 0) {
      Object.assign(where, baseFilters);
    }

    const rows: DbConversationRow[] = await this.engine.find(CONVERSATIONS_OBJECT, {
      where: Object.keys(where).length > 0 ? where : undefined,
      orderBy: [
        { field: 'created_at', order: 'asc' },
        { field: 'id', order: 'asc' },
      ],
```

`list()` loads messages per conversation sequentially (`await` inside a loop), which adds latency proportional to the page size. Even if N+1 is acceptable, running the per-conversation message fetches in parallel (e.g., `Promise.all`) would significantly reduce wall-clock time.
```suggestion
    const conversations: AIConversation[] = await Promise.all(
      rows.map(async (row) => {
        const messages: DbMessageRow[] = await this.engine.find(MESSAGES_OBJECT, {
          where: { conversation_id: row.id },
          orderBy: [{ field: 'created_at', order: 'asc' }],
        });
        return this.toConversation(row, messages);
      }),
    );
```

`list()` implements cursor-based pagination, but there is no test covering the `cursor` option (including ordering + filter correctness). Add at least one test that creates multiple conversations, pages with `cursor`, and asserts no skips/duplicates and stable ordering.
```suggestion
    // We use a total order (created_at ASC, id ASC) and select rows that are
    // strictly after the cursor in that order to avoid skips/duplicates.
    if (options.cursor) {
      const cursorRow = await this.engine.findOne(CONVERSATIONS_OBJECT, {
        where: { id: options.cursor },
        fields: ['created_at', 'id'],
      });
      if (cursorRow) {
        // (created_at > cursor.created_at) OR
        // (created_at = cursor.created_at AND id > cursor.id)
        (where as Record<string, unknown>)['$or'] = [
          { created_at: { $gt: cursorRow.created_at } },
          { created_at: cursorRow.created_at, id: { $gt: cursorRow.id } },
        ];
      }
    }

    const rows: DbConversationRow[] = await this.engine.find(CONVERSATIONS_OBJECT, {
      where: Object.keys(where).length > 0 ? where : undefined,
      orderBy: [
